[
  {
  "title": "Global Threat Intelligence Lab – End-to-End SOC & Automation Project (Sentinel-Feed-Threat Intelligence)",
  "thumbnail": "https://files.elfsightcdn.com/eafe4a4d-3436-495d-b748-5bdce62d911d/5c8f3a6b-46b5-4356-a3e7-29691b55fe64/Screenshot-2025-09-10-at-00-21-42.png",
  "description": "Automated cloud-based threat detection pipeline using AWS-native services and generative AI. Real-time monitoring, LLM analysis, MITRE ATT&CK mapping, and Slack alerts.",
  "long_description": "I designed and built a fully functional Global Threat Intelligence Lab from scratch, combining open-source tools, cloud resources, and custom Python automation to simulate enterprise-level threat detection and response workflows. The lab demonstrates a complete threat intelligence pipeline—from collecting raw indicators of compromise (IoCs) to enriching them with context, storing them in MISP, and feeding alerts into SIEM systems like Wazuh and Azure Sentinel.\n\nKey Highlights:\n- Threat Feed Aggregation: Integrated 8+ OSINT feeds including AlienVault OTX, Abuse.ch, PhishTank, Emerging Threats, and blocklist.de. Automated normalization of heterogeneous data into a unified JSON schema for consistent processing.\n- IoC Enrichment: Leveraged VirusTotal, Shodan, AbuseIPDB, and MaxMind GeoIP to provide actionable context for every indicator, including reputation scores, geolocation, ASN, open ports, and CVEs. Implemented caching strategies to respect API rate limits and ensure efficiency.\n- MISP Integration: Automated ingestion of enriched IoCs into MISP using PyMISP, creating daily events with detailed threat context. Handled authentication, SSL, and containerization challenges on Docker to ensure a stable environment.\n- SIEM Integration & Alerts: Pushed IoCs to Wazuh and Azure Sentinel, triggering real-time alerts for simulated malicious activity. Created custom rules and watchlists to demonstrate detection of attacks in a controlled lab setting.\n- Custom SOC Dashboard: Developed a Flask-based dashboard with real-time logging, pipeline status, and interactive geo-maps of attacker IPs. Implemented Flask-SocketIO for live updates and visual monitoring.\n- Automation & Reliability: Scheduled regular feed collection and enrichment via cron jobs. Implemented robust error handling, retries, and logging for uninterrupted pipeline execution.\n\nLearning & Career Impact: Strengthened practical skills in Python automation, SOC workflows, threat intelligence processing, and SIEM integration. The project serves as a strong portfolio demonstration for roles in cybersecurity, SOC engineering, and threat intelligence.\n\nProject Challenges:\n- Handling inconsistent feed formats and API rate limits.\n- Docker deployment on ARM architecture required troubleshooting and custom port mappings.\n- Git mishap led to loss of some scripts, reinforcing the importance of version control and backups.\n\nOutcome:\n- Collected and enriched 2,000+ IoCs per run, integrated across multiple feeds.\n- Real-time alerts generated in Wazuh/Sentinel during simulated attacks.\n- Fully operational, automated SOC pipeline with visualization, providing practical exposure to enterprise-grade threat intelligence workflows.\n\nThis project demonstrates my ability to design, implement, and manage end-to-end threat detection systems using free and open-source tools, highlighting technical creativity, automation skills, and operational security expertise.",
  "skills": ["GuardDuty", "AWS", "IAM", "Cloud Security", "Lambda", "MITRE ATT&CK", "AI/LLM Integration", "Amazon Bedrock"],
  "link": "https://medium.com/@lakshan.sam28/i-built-a-global-threat-intel-lab-from-scratch-then-deleted-half-of-it-by-accident-68ccf22fdb1c",
  "category": "Defensive - Blue Teaming",
  "date": "2025-10-01",
