  {
    "title": "AI-Powered Threat Detection Pipeline using AWS GuardDuty, Lambda & Amazon Bedrock - Full Walkthrough & Technical Breakdown",
    "thumbnail": "https://files.elfsightcdn.com/eafe4a4d-3436-495d-b748-5bdce62d911d/eacce538-52a2-40a3-9442-87f3f1e59817/Screenshot-2025-08-02-at-17-46-47.png",
    "description": "This project involved designing and deploying a fully automated cloud-based threat detection pipeline using AWS-native services combined with generative AI. The primary objective was to build a real-time monitoring system that listens to GuardDuty findings, analyzes them using Amazon Bedrock’s large language models (LLMs), maps them to MITRE ATT&CK techniques, and sends structured threat intelligence alerts to a Slack workspace. To begin with, Amazon GuardDuty was enabled to continuously monitor AWS resources for suspicious behavior. This included brute-force SSH attacks on EC2 instances, anomalous API calls, and cryptocurrency mining activity. Once a threat was detected by GuardDuty, an EventBridge rule forwarded the finding directly to an AWS Lambda function. This function served as the central analysis engine in the pipeline. The Lambda function, written in Python, parsed the incoming GuardDuty JSON payload, extracted relevant fields such as threat type, description, IP addresses, and severity score. It then used Amazon Bedrock to interpret and summarize the threat in human-readable language. The Lambda logic also mapped the finding to a relevant MITRE ATT&CK technique using a custom mapping dictionary. Based on the nature of the finding, the function determined actionable steps, such as isolating an EC2 instance or blocking specific IP addresses in security groups. For operational visibility, the Lambda function was configured to send a structured message to a Slack channel using Slack’s incoming webhooks. The alert format included the threat type, severity score, MITRE technique, affected IP addresses, a recommended action, and a timestamp. This allowed for immediate awareness and incident triage within a team communication tool without needing to check the AWS Console. All necessary IAM roles and policies were carefully configured to follow the principle of least privilege. The Lambda function was granted permissions to invoke Bedrock models, write logs to CloudWatch, and be triggered by EventBridge. Additional logging and error handling mechanisms ensured that failures in any part of the pipeline would be logged and visible for troubleshooting. A major challenge in this project was ensuring that real (not simulated) GuardDuty findings were properly triggered. To achieve this, the project included generating real-world SSH brute-force attempts against a deliberately exposed EC2 instance. This verified that the detection, analysis, and alerting pipeline worked end to end in a live environment. Debugging IAM misconfigurations, Slack integration issues, and proper parsing of GuardDuty payloads took several hours of iteration and testing, but the outcome was a reliable and reusable security automation workflow. This project demonstrates hands-on proficiency in building serverless architectures on AWS, applying LLMs for cybersecurity automation, integrating cloud detection tools with communication platforms, and working with real-time security event data. You can read the full technical breakdown, implementation walkthrough, and see all supporting screenshots in the Medium article linked below: Read the full article:",
